WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.740
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:04.720
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.610
Anyone who has tried building bridges

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:09.339
over a huge chasm realized that it is

00:00:09.339 --> 00:00:11.799
possibly one of the most difficult *and*

00:00:11.799 --> 00:00:13.419
dangerous things you could do on a

00:00:13.419 --> 00:00:15.280
family vacation. The basic construction

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:17.650
elements for such a bridge can be ropes,

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:20.500
cables, and wires. And this kind of task

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:21.939
is fundamentally different from

00:00:21.939 --> 00:00:23.859
classical architectural building

00:00:23.859 --> 00:00:26.050
problems. Here, you don't need to have any

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:28.330
kind of scaffolding or to carry building

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:29.589
blocks that weigh a lot.

00:00:29.589 --> 00:00:31.779
However, you have to know how to tie

00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:33.730
knots therefore this is the kind of

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:35.680
problem you'll need flying machines for.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:37.690
They can fly anywhere. They are nimble.

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:39.610
And their disadvantage that they have a

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:41.860
very limited payload does not play a big

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:44.200
role here. In this piece of work at the

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.570
ETH Zürich these machines can create some

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:49.780
crazy knots: from single to multi-round-turn hitches,

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.840
knobs, elbows, round turns

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.640
and multiple rope knobs. And these you

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:56.050
have to be able to create in a

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:58.120
collaborative manner because each

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:00.700
individual flying machine will hold one rope,

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.020
therefore they have to pass through

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.330
given control points at the strictly

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:07.440
given time and a target velocity.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.000
These little guys also have to know the exact

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.830
amount of force they need to exert on

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:13.930
the structure to move into a desirable

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:16.600
target position. Even deploying the rope

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:18.730
is not that trivial. The machine is

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:20.920
equipped with a roller to do so, but the

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:22.750
friction of this roller can be changed

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:24.700
at any time according to the rope

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.480
releasing direction to unroll it properly.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:28.960
You also have to face the correct

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:30.970
direction as well. And these structures

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:32.260
are not just toys.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:33.790
The resulting bridges are resilient

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:36.970
enough for humans to use. This work is a

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:38.620
great example to show that the

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:40.870
technology of today is improving at an

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:42.760
incredible pace. If we can solve

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:45.370
difficult collaborative control problems

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:48.460
such as this one just think about the possibilities..

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:51.250
What an exciting time it is to be alive!

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:53.409
We have gotten lots of shares for the

00:01:53.409 --> 00:01:55.479
series on social media. I'm trying to

00:01:55.479 --> 00:01:56.320
send a short

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.180
thank you message for every single one

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:00.100
of you. I'm trying my best and don't

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:02.590
forget: every single share helps spreading

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:04.570
the word for the series immensely.

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:11.580
Thanks for watching and I'll see you next time!

